2. @Configuration: 
--> @Configuration classes are processed during Spring Boot startup to register beans in the application context,
    before other dependent beans are created.

🔹 Key Points
--> @Configuration classes are processed first to register @Bean methods.
--> Beans inside @Configuration are singleton by default and managed by Spring.
--> @Component beans are created after scanning, so if they depend on a @Bean, it must already exist.
--> @Configuration ensures that even if a @Bean method is called multiple times, same singleton instance is returned (thanks to CGLIB proxy).
Example : 
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public MyService myService() {
        return new MyServiceImpl();
    }
}

@Component
public class MyComponent {

    @Autowired
    private MyService myService; // injected from AppConfig
}
✅ Here:
--> AppConfig (@Configuration) runs first, creates myService bean
--> MyComponent (@Component) is initialized after, and receives the same singleton myService

3. @Bean : 
--> It tells Spring to create and manage an object (bean) returned by a method.

Why @Bean is used : 
--> When you cannot annotate the class with @Component (third-party / library class)
--> When you want full control over object creation
Ex: 
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }
}
✅ Spring creates one instance of PasswordEncoder
✅ Available for @Autowired anywhere

How it works (Simple)
--> Spring internally does: PasswordEncoder encoder = passwordEncoder();
    and stores it in the ApplicationContext.

