10. @Primary : 
=================
--> It tells Spring which bean should be injected by default when multiple beans of the same type exist.

🧠 Why @Primary is used
--> To resolve ambiguity when more than one bean of the same type is present
--> Avoids NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

Problem Example (Without @Primary) : 
@Component
public class SmsService implements NotificationService {}

@Component
public class EmailService implements NotificationService {}

@Autowired
private NotificationService notificationService;

❌ Spring error: NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

✅ Solution Using @Primary : 
@Component
@Primary
public class EmailService implements NotificationService {}
--> Spring injects EmailService by default

🔁 How it works
  1. Spring sees multiple beans
  2. Picks the one marked with @Primary

⚠️ Important Notes
  1. Works only when multiple beans of same type
  2. Can be overridden using @Qualifier
  3. Applicable on @Component / @Bean

11. @Qualifier : 
--> @Qualifier is used to specify which bean to inject when multiple beans of the same type are available.

🧠 Why @Qualifier is used
--> To remove ambiguity
--> To select a specific implementation
--> Overrides @Primary

🔧 Problem Scenario
 1. interface : 
public interface PaymentService {}

UpiPaymentService: 
--------------------
@Component("upiService")
public class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService {}

CardPaymentService: 
---------------------
@Component("cardService")
public class CardPaymentService implements PaymentService {}

❌ Without @Qualifier
------------------------
@Autowired
private PaymentService paymentService;

❌ Error: NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

✅ With @Qualifier : 
-----------------------
@Autowired
@Qualifier("upiService")
private PaymentService paymentService;

--> ✔ UpiPaymentService is injected

Q: 🆚 @Qualifier vs @Primary (Interview-Ready) : 

| Feature        | `@Qualifier`                 | `@Primary`              |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Purpose        | Select **specific bean**     | Select **default bean** |
| When used      | At **injection point**       | On **bean definition**  |
| Control        | **Explicit** (local)         | **Implicit** (global)   |
| Multiple beans | Yes                          | Yes (only one primary)  |
| Overrides      | Overrides `@Primary`         | Can be overridden       |
| Best use case  | Different behavior per class | One common default      |
| Scope          | Per injection                | Application-wide        |

12. @PostConstruct : 
--> It runs a method automatically after the bean is created and all dependencies are injected.

🧠 Why @PostConstruct is used
--> To perform initialization logic
--> To validate configuration
--> To load cache / pre-data after startup

🔄 Bean Lifecycle (Simple) : 
Bean created
   ↓
Dependencies injected (@Autowired)
   ↓
@PostConstruct method runs  ✅

Ex: 
@Component
public class CacheLoader {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("Cache loaded");
    }
}
--> Runs once at application startup
--> Runs after DI, not before

⚠️ Important Rules
1. Method must be: I. void.  II. No arguments
2. Only one per bean (recommended)
3. Runs only once

Q: 🆚 @PostConstruct vs Constructor ?

| Feature                | Constructor            | `@PostConstruct` |
| ---------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------- |
| DI completed           | ❌                     | ✅              |
| Access autowired beans | ❌                     | ✅              |
| Called                 | During object creation  | After injection |

13. @PreDestroy : 
--> It runs a method just before a Spring bean is destroyed (when the application is shutting down).

🧠 Why @PreDestroy is used
--> To perform cleanup logic
--> Release resources
--> Close connections gracefully

🔄 Bean Lifecycle (Complete View) : 

Bean created
   ↓
Dependencies injected
   ↓
@PostConstruct
   ↓
Application running
   ↓
@PreDestroy  ✅
   ↓
Bean destroyed

Ex: 
@Component
public class ResourceManager {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("Resources initialized");
    }

    @PreDestroy
    public void cleanup() {
        System.out.println("Resources released");
    }
}
--> ✔ Runs once
--> ✔ Triggered during graceful shutdown

⚠️ Important Rules

Method must be: 1. void  2. No arguments

Executes only if: 1. Context closes normally 2. Not on JVM crash

Q: 🆚 @PreDestroy vs @PostConstruct ?

| Feature | `@PostConstruct`    | `@PreDestroy`           |
| ------- | ------------------- | ----------------------- |
| When    | After bean creation | Before bean destruction |
| Use     | Initialization      | Cleanup                 |

14. @Scope : 
--> @Scope defines how and when Spring creates bean instances.

🧠 Why @Scope is used
--> Control bean instance creation
--> Define whether a bean is shared or new per request

Common Bean Scopes : 
| Scope                 | Meaning                           |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `singleton` (default) | One instance per Spring container |
| `prototype`           | New instance every time           |
| `request`             | One per HTTP request              |
| `session`             | One per HTTP session              |
| `application`         | One per web application           |
| `websocket`           | One per WebSocket                 |

Ex: Singleton (Default)
@Component
@Scope("singleton")
public class UserService {
}
--> ✔ Same object reused

Ex: Prototype
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class TokenGenerator {
}
--> ✔ New object every injection

Ex: Web Scope
@Component
@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST)
public class RequestData {
}

⚠️ Important Interview Points
--> Singleton beans live from startup to shutdown

--> Prototype beans:
           1. Spring creates
           2. Spring does NOT destroy
           3. @PreDestroy ❌ for prototype

